  { q: 'Do ravens bring death?', a: 'No. Ravens do not cause or predict death. They are attracted to carrion — dead animals — as a food source. If a raven appears near a dying animal or a human settlement with a death, it is responding to environmental cues (smell, the presence of other scavengers, exposed carcasses) rather than causing or foretelling the event.' },
  { q: 'Why do people think ravens are connected to death?', a: 'Ravens are carrion birds — they eat dead animals. Before modern sanitation, ravens were common at battlefields, plague pits, public executions, and slaughterhouses. The consistent association of ravens with death-associated places created the cultural link. The raven was a witness to death, not its cause.' },
